  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1132 PM CDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nThunderstorms are ongoing this evening and will become more\nnumerous overnight and into the morning across the forecast \narea. Locally heavy rainfall, leading to ponding on roads and \nelevated streams is a possibility in the area with the heaviest \nrainfall. Storms will gradually diminish from northwest to \nsoutheast across the area through mid morning.\n\nTemperatures are going to be noticeable cooler today with\nwidespread max temperatures in the low to mid 80s. Precipitation\nchances are negligible as well given we will be in between \nwaves. Low temperatures Thursday night will fall into the low to\nmid 60s area wide. With no systems in the vicinity, winds will \nalso be on the light side, though predominantly out of the \nsouth and southeastward direction Thursday and Friday.\n\nThe next appreciable chance at any kind of rain and storm\nchances will be across our eastern counties late Friday evening\nand into Saturday morning. There are mixed solutions among the\nglobal and short range models regarding the potential for an MCS\nto move southward through central Kansas. The GFS and ENS-\nControl are bullish on the idea, with HRRR, RRFS, and NAMNest\nshowing very little in the way of convection. I would tend to \nside more on the CAM solutions, but the latest NBM has 20-40% \nPOPs for eastern counties, with the greatest chances farther \neast. So we will at least maintain mention of chance \nthunderstorms in the forecast. Finally, the next chance POPs \ncome Monday-Wednesday of next week when troughing become more of\na dominant pattern regime over the central and northern Plains.",
